$\begin{aligned}
& \text{We have, } \frac{a \cos A+b \cos B+c \cos C}{a+b+c}\\
& =\frac{R(\sin 2 A+\sin 2 B+\sin 2 C)}{2 R(\sin A+\sin B+\sin C)}\\
& =\frac{4 \sin A \sin B \sin C}{2\left(4 \cos \frac{A}{2} \cos \frac{B}{2} \cos \frac{C}{2}\right)}: \\
& =4 \sin \frac{A}{2} \sin \frac{B}{2} \sin \frac{C}{2}=\frac{r}{R}
\end{aligned}\)$

Since the normal at $\left(a p^{2}, 2 a p\right)$ on $y^{2}=4 a x$ meets the curve again at $\left(\mathrm{aq}^{2}, 2 \mathrm{aq}\right)$, therefore $\mathrm{px}+\mathrm{y}=2 \mathrm{ap}+\mathrm{ap}^{3}$ passes through $\left(\mathrm{aq}^{2}, 2 \mathrm{aq}\right)$\begin{array}{l}
\Rightarrow $\mathrm{paq}^{2}+$2 $\mathrm{aq}=$2 $\mathrm{ap}+\mathrm{ap}^{3}$ \\
\Rightarrow $\mathrm{p}\left(\mathrmq^{2}-\mathrmp^{2}\right)=2(\mathrm{p}$-$\mathrm{q}$) \\
\Rightarrow $\mathrm{p}$($\mathrm{q}+\mathrm{p})=$-2 \\
\Rightarrow \mathrmp^{2}+$\mathrm{pq}+$2=0
\end{array}
